The provided code for the `Queue` class has a subtle bug in the `deque` method. Specifically, when the last element is dequeued, the `_tail` is not reset to `undefined`, leading to an inconsistent state. After dequeuing the last element, the `_head` is correctly set to `null`, but the `_tail` still points to the now-removed node. This causes issues when subsequently enqueueing new elements, as the `_head` remains `null`, making the queue appear empty even though elements are added.

To fix this, the `deque` method should check if the new `_head` is `null` (indicating the queue is now empty) and reset `_tail` to `null` in that case. Here's the corrected `deque` method:

```typescript
deque(): T | undefined {
    if (!this._head) {
        return undefined;
    }

    this.length--;

    const head = this._head;
    this._head = this._head.next;

    // Reset tail if the queue is now empty
    if (!this._head) {
        this._tail = undefined;
    }

    head.next = undefined;
    return head.value;
}
```

